I'll implement a **MinMaxStack** that tracks both minimum and maximum in O(1), using parallel tracking arrays:

```java
public class MinMaxStack {
    private int[] values;
    private int[] mins;      // mins[i] = min from index 0 to i
    private int[] maxs;      // maxs[i] = max from index 0 to i
    private int size;
    private static final int INITIAL_CAPACITY = 16;

    public MinMaxStack() {
        values = new int[INITIAL_CAPACITY];
        mins = new int[INITIAL_CAPACITY];
        maxs = new int[INITIAL_CAPACITY];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        if (size == values.length) {
            resize();
        }

        values[size] = value;
        mins[size] = (size == 0) ? value : Math.min(mins[size - 1], value);
        maxs[size] = (size == 0) ? value : Math.max(maxs[size - 1], value);
        size++;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack underflow");
        }
        return values[--size];
    }

    public int getMin() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return mins[size - 1];
    }

    public int getMax() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return maxs[size - 1];
    }

    private void resize() {
        int newCapacity = values.length * 2;
        int[] newValues = new int[newCapacity];
        int[] newMins = new int[newCapacity];
        int[] newMaxs = new int[newCapacity];

        System.arraycopy(values, 0, newValues, 0, size);
        System.arraycopy(mins, 0, newMins, 0, size);
        System.arraycopy(maxs, 0, newMaxs, 0, size);

        values = newValues;
        mins = newMins;
        maxs = newMaxs;
    }
}
```

**Why min/max?** It's more interesting than a single value—you get *both* extremes simultaneously. Each new element only needs two comparisons, and the parallel arrays maintain cumulative extrema with zero extra space overhead (amortized). The monotonic invariant `mins[i] ≤ mins[i+1]` and `maxs[i] ≥ maxs[i+1]` makes queries trivial.
